{"created": "2026-10-02T11:43:38.964023+00:00", "modified": "2026-10-02T15:13:01.813259+00:00", "id": 23101928, "conceptrecid": "22986529", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23101928", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.22986529", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23101928", "metadata": {"title": "One Object, Three Descriptions: Axes, Images and Grids of a Jacquard Pattern System", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.23101928", "publication_date": "2026-10-02", "description": "<p><span>&lt;p&gt;A Jacquard pattern system has been described in three separate ways: as sixteen families of straight axes on a 12 &times; 12 square combined by a parity rule (note [A], doi:10.5281/zenodo.22965031); as sixty three-tint images from which 512 grids are built by a six-bit selection (paper [G], data doi:10.5281/zenodo.22862110); and as level sets of a square-plate eigenmode (note [P], same deposit as [A]). This paper shows, by exhaustive check on the published data, that the three descriptions present one finite structure (the sense is made precise in &sect;1).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Theorem 1 (finite-corpus identification).&lt;/strong&gt; Every one of the sixty images is a parity figure of axes: forty-eight cells of lines, the same for all sixty and equal to the nodal set of the degenerate guided plate mode cos(4&pi;x/a) &minus; cos(4&pi;y/a); ninety-six cells carrying the parity of a union of the four families of level T0; and one of four tint permutations, which are the four natures of the system. The fifteen families of the loom are the fifteen non-empty subsets of the four T0 families (60/60 verified). &lt;strong&gt;Corollary.&lt;/strong&gt; The half-shift theorem of [G] &mdash; exactly eight families of fifteen admit unified pairs, those with exactly one base of yang type &mdash; follows in two lines. &lt;strong&gt;Theorem 2.&lt;/strong&gt; The parity map is injective on the 70 band systems visible on the triangle subdivision, and the sixteen family figures span a [1152, 13] binary code of minimum distance 288 with a unique minimum-weight word, T0 YANG MUT&nbsp;</span><span>&oplus;</span><span> T1 YIN (parity counted from the origin corner); on the cell reading the code is [144, 12] of distance 36. &lt;strong&gt;Theorem 3.&lt;/strong&gt; On levels, the doubling homothety terminates without cycles if and only if the period is 2^a or 3&middot;2^a, has a non-trivial fixed level (120&deg;) if and only if 3 divides the period, and 12 is the smallest period with both and depth at least two.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Nothing is measured on a plate; the plate reading is exact for guided edges and approximate for free ones. Every number is printed by one of five scripts from &lt;code&gt;catalogue-axes.json&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;referent_360_v3.json&lt;/code&gt;, both included, together with the six figures (SVG) and the script that draws them.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Licences.&lt;/strong&gt; Text, figures and data: CC BY 4.0. Code: AGPL v3; commercial licence on request.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Version 1.2.0.&lt;/strong&gt; Revised after reading: (1) what \\\"one object\\\" means is defined in &sect;1, and the independence of the corpora is stated there ([G] was generated and classified before the axes were formalised); (2) Theorem 1 is labelled a finite-corpus identification and Theorem 2 a finite computation; (3) new Proposition 1: the four T0 families are a basis of F_2^4, embedded injectively by the parity map (rank 4, rank 5 with the all-dark figure; new script &lt;code&gt;espace_F2_4.py&lt;/code&gt;), and the half-shift acts through a linear form, so the eight unified families are the complement of a hyperplane, 2^4 &minus; 2^3; (4) the plate section is shortened and separates \\\"exact in the guided model\\\" from \\\"predicted, not measured\\\"; (5) new &sect;6 on the two referents (256 and 360) used by the Carter encoding, as a use of the structure and not its origin; (6) &sect;7 retitled \\\"Why 12 is the smallest admissible period\\\", with its three criteria stated; (7) &sect;8 is a table sorting every statement by status. (8) &sect;5: the dimension 13 is derived from the levels (5 diagonal + 8 orthogonal); the C1 reading now states its rule for axes through cell centres (continuous closure on the side of increasing coordinate, as in regle_parite.py of [A]), on which every C1 number depends &mdash; the previous text wrongly said only two keys were affected. (9) &sect;7: the proof of Theorem 3 is read further &mdash; for any period N = 2^a M, the descent is a set of cycles of lengths r(d) = min{r : 2^r &equiv; &plusmn;1 mod d}, one family per divisor d &gt; 1 of M, with trees of height a above them; it terminates exactly when every r(d) = 1, i.e. M </span><span>&isin;</span><span> {1, 3}; the table gives the cycles for N = 10 and 18, and harmonie_N.py checks the structure for every even N &le; 200. No number changed.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Version 1.2.1.&lt;/strong&gt; Licence of the text, figures and data set to CC BY 4.0 (it read CC BY-NC 4.0 in 1.2.0); the general study of doubling modulo n is cited (doi:10.5281/zenodo.23088601). No other change.&lt;/p&gt;</span></p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Amiot, Anibal Edelberto", "affiliation": null, "orcid": "0009-0002-6414-9448"}], "keywords": ["Jacquard weaving", "parity figures", "linear codes", "level sets", "square plate eigenmodes", "Chladni figures", "half-shift criterion", "doubling map", "arithmogeometry"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.22965031", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.22862110", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "software", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.22866061", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "software", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.22945021", "relation": "isDocumentedBy", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.22722485", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "publication-book", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.22967585", "relation": "isReferencedBy", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}, {"identifier": "https://github.com/anibaledel/livreedhermes", "relation": "isSupplementTo", "resource_type": "software", "scheme": "url"}, {"identifier": "10.5281/zenodo.23088601", "relation": "references", "resource_type": "publication-preprint", "scheme": "doi"}], "version": "1.2.1", "language": "eng", "custom": {"code:codeRepository": "https://github.com/anibaledel/livreedhermes"}, "resource_type": {"title": "Preprint", "type": "publication", "subtype": "preprint"}, "alternate_identifiers": [{"identifier": "Q141191562"}], "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 2, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "22986529"}}]}, "notes": "<p><span>Code under AGPL v3. Commercial licence: anibaledel@gmail.com</span></p>"}, "title": "One Object, Three Descriptions: Axes, Images and Grids of a Jacquard Pattern System", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23101928", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/23101928", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/23101928?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23101928", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23101928", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.23101928", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23101928/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/22986529", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/22986529", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22986529", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.22986529", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23101928/manifest", "self_iiif_sequence": "https://zenodo.org/api/iiif/record:23101928/sequence/default", "files": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/23101928/fil